Reading the model as D-B-M-L.

A finished model needs to be written down — precisely, shareably, and in something a tool can render. The Jabra warehouse is captured as DBML — Database Markup Language: a concise text notation for tables and their relationships, which renders to an E-R diagram at dbdiagram.io.

Let's look at the syntax. You write "Table FactSales", then open a brace, and each line inside is a column: its name, its type, and optional tags in brackets. So sales key, integer, primary key — the surrogate. Order-date key, customer key, product key — integers, not null. Order i-d, integer, with a note that it's a degenerate dimension. Then quantity, and line total. You write dim customer the same way — customer key as the primary key, customer i-d noted as the natural key, then name and region. And finally, outside the tables, a relationship line: "Ref: FactSales dot customer key, greater-than, DimCustomer dot customer key."

So how do you read it? A Table block, in braces, is one table, and each line is a column, with its type and optional tags. Primary key marks the surrogate P-K; not null marks the required foreign keys. A note documents intent — which columns are degenerate dimensions, which are natural keys, which are SCD-2. And a Ref line is one edge of the star: the fact's foreign key — the greater-than sign meaning many-to-one — pointing to the dimension's primary key. Read all the Ref lines, and you've read the star's shape.

Why use DBML? Three reasons. It's version-controllable and reviewable — the model is text, so it diffs and reviews like code. It's the single source of the physical model — one file defines the tables, keys, and relationships. And it renders an E-R diagram — paste it into dbdiagram.io for the picture, with no manual drawing. The full model lives in the file jabra-spain-dw-model dot dbml — the star from this whole module, made concrete and shareable. So: DBML captures the finished model as reviewable text — Table blocks are tables, primary-key marks the surrogate P-Ks, and each Ref is a foreign-key-to-primary-key edge of the star — a version-controlled source of truth that renders straight to an E-R diagram.
